---
import Tutorial from '../../components/Tutorial.astro'
import Graph from '../../components/Graph.astro'
import source from '../../../../examples/react/main.go?raw'
import { nav } from '../../data/tutorials'
const { prev, next } = nav('react')
const output = `Question: What is twice the population of France?
Thought: I need France's population before I can double it.
Action: lookup[population of France]
Observation: 68000000
Thought: Now I double the population I found.
Action: calculate[68000000 * 2]
Observation: 136000000
Final Answer: Twice the population of France is 136000000.`
---
  <Tutorial title="ReAct: Thought, Action, Observation" pattern="ReAct" primitives={['Router', 'Stream', 'Node']}
    slug="react" source={source} output={output} prev={prev} next={next}>
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p class="lede">The canonical tool-using agent: the model reasons, names a tool, sees the result, and repeats until it has a final answer.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="story">
      <p>
        ReAct is two nodes. <code>reason</code> asks the model for one step: either a Thought plus an Action, or a Final
        Answer. <code>act</code> runs the named tool and writes the Observation into the trace. The router after
        <code>reason</code> sends the run to <code>End</code> when an answer exists and to <code>act</code> otherwise.
      </p>
      <p>
        The interesting part is the <code>LLM</code> type: <code>func(ctx, prompt string) (string, error)</code>. The
        example ships a scripted mock that answers by counting completed turns. Replace it with a real completion call and
        the graph, the parser, and the tools stay exactly as they are. Tool failures are written into the trace as
        observations too, so the model can react to them instead of the run dying.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="graph">
      <Graph w={460} h={190}
        nodes={[{id:'reason',x:80,y:70},{id:'act',x:250,y:70},{id:'end',x:410,y:70,kind:'end'}]}
        edges={[{from:'reason',to:'act',label:'Action'},{from:'act',to:'reason',label:'Observation',bend:55},{from:'reason',to:'end',label:'Final Answer',bend:-70}]} />
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="notes">
      <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>What to notice.</strong> The prompt is rebuilt from the state on every turn (<code>prompt(s)</code>), so the state <em>is</em> the memory. There is no hidden message list inside the engine; whatever you keep in the struct is what the model sees.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="callout go">
        <p><strong>Shared slices.</strong> <code>act</code> edits the last turn through a pointer into <code>s.Trace</code>. That is fine in a sequential graph, but a previously yielded <code>Step</code> shares that backing array. If you keep old Steps around (history, time travel), copy the slice before mutating it.</p>
      </div>
      <h2>Make it real</h2>
      <p>Swap <code>mockLLM</code> for a function that calls your provider (see <a href="/tutorials/llm">Swap in a real model</a>) and put real tools in the <code>tools</code> map. Keep the parser strict: a model that emits neither an Action nor a Final Answer should produce an error, which stops the run at a resumable Step instead of looping on garbage.</p>
    </Fragment>
  </Tutorial>
